{"find": "[[page ?]]\n[[blank]]", "replace": "[[page ?]]\n[[cont]] differed mainly from the rough branching of the Coptic. Indeed, the evidence of a Greek equivalent seems to have been borrowed, to suggest that Libyan letters were gathered in the course of the travels of the people between all those countries round the shores of the Mediterranean, and probably, by their traders as well, in direct communication with the Arabs of Africa.\n\nSome of these Tuareg letters have, of course, remarkably altered from the Libyan form from which they seem to have been derived, but it can be maintained that probably nearly 2,000 years have elapsed since their first adoption, and that during the whole of that time there has been little or no use of even written books to aid the preservation of the original forms; it cannot be said that the groups of words have differentiated from the original greatly, though the alphabet has preserved quite few in similar cases."}